+# PRODUCT_UX_SPEC.md — NC MANAGER V1.21
+
+> **Document type:** Product / UX Definition (not implementation).
+> **Status:** Living specification reflecting the owner's current product & UX requirements.
+> **Scope:** Defines the *intended professional product experience*. It does **not** modify source, does not redesign, does not remove features, does not add dependencies.
+> **Priority rule:** Owner's explicit requirements are the source of truth. Whatever is **not** explicitly defined is marked **`OPEN DECISION`** — never invented.
+> **Do not emulate other products:** This document must not assume NC MANAGER should behave like ProNest, Lantek, SigmaNEST, AutoCAD, Tekla, etc. unless the owner says so.
+
+---
+
+## 0. How to read this document
+
+- Sections follow the required output structure (1–29).
+- `OPEN DECISION` marks a point where the owner has **not** defined the requirement yet, and a decision must be made **later** before implementation. It is not a design choice we make here.
+- "Never break" items in §26 are non-negotiable infrastructure guarantees.
+- The RED rule: this spec is a **definition**, not a build order. Implementation of any item requires a separate approved step.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Product Identity
+
+**Name (canonical, owner-mandated):** `NC Manager V1.21`
+
+- The application title must **consistently** read **"NC Manager V1.21"**.
+- It must **not** appear as "NC Manager 1.v21", nor be duplicated/inconsistent across windows, dialogs, headers, about boxes, or packaging.
+- **`OPEN DECISION`:** exact the prefix "V" placement in every surface (title bar, header, about dialog) — the canonical string is fixed above; the sole point is to apply it *uniformly* everywhere.
+
+**Product class:** A **professional industrial fabrication-file manager** (not a demo, not a prototype, not a mockup, not a generic template, not "a collection of unrelated dialogs").
+
+**Perceived quality bar:** Every UI decision must follow human/workflow logic. Professional means **clear, predictable, coherent, efficient, precise, industrial, trustworthy, consistent, easy to understand, safe for real work**. It does **not** mean more buttons, animations, decoration, or complexity.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Real Purpose of NC MANAGER
+
+NC MANAGER **reads and manages industrial fabrication files**.
+
+Supported / targeted formats (current and future):
+- **NC**
+- **NC1**
+- **DXF**
+- **DWG**
+
+The product must be able to:
+- read / import files
+- understand their geometry and fabrication information
+- display them in **2D**
+- display them in **3D** where supported
+- display **blueprint-style** views
+- inspect **holes** and **slots**
+- modify supported geometry / data
+- organize **files / projects**
+- eventually **generate / export** NC/NC1/DXF/DWG
+
+**Original reason for existence (owner):**
+A user receives **many NC/NC1 files exported from Tekla**. The program must make it easy to bring those files into the program and **organize them intelligently** — as a real industrial file-management workflow, not a demo feature.
+
+Representative profile examples the organization flow must handle:
+`HEA100, HEA120, IPE, PL10, PL15, PL20, L70, …`
+
+**Future generation direction:** The owner explicitly wants the application to become capable of **generating NC/NC1/DXF/DWG** in the future (§22 stores this direction only — no feature invented).
+
+---
+
+## 3. Target User / Human Workflow
+
+**Primary user (inferred from owner context, confirmed by de facto usage):**
+An engineer / workshop user in a steel fabrication bureau (`bureau d'études` / chaudronnerie–charpente métallique) who:
+- receives NC/NC1/DXF files exported from Tekla,
+- needs to bring them in, organize them by project/order/profile,
+- inspect each part (geometry, holes, slots, orientation),
+- correct supported geometry/data,
+- produce organized, traceable output for the machine / the workshop.
+
+**Workflow posture (human logic, not tool logic):**
+1. **Receive** files from the design office.
+2. **Bring them in** (import) — safely, on copies, never touching originals.
+3. **Organize** — by project/order, family, category.
+4. **Inspect** — 2D / 3D / blueprint, holes & slots.
+5. **Correct** — supported edits with undo.
+6. **Produce / export / print** — eventually; the path to generation is a stated direction.
+
+**`OPEN DECISION`:** Whether there are distinct roles (e.g. "workshop operator" vs "bureau engineer") with different default workspaces. Not defined; left open.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Core Workflow
+
+The core loop (current working functionality) that the UX must support and make feel industrial:
+
+```
+Import (NC/NC1/DXF/(DWG future))  →  Organize by Project/Order
+        →  Inspect (Files table → Viewer 2D/3D/Blueprint → Holes & Slots)
+        →  Modify (holes/slots, supported data) with Undo/Dirty state
+        →  Save / Open Project (flat storage, original file names)
+        →  Export (NC1/NC/DXF/OBJ/STL/PDF) — and, in the future, generation
+```
+
+Notable human-workflow expectations:
+- **Search directly below the project selector** (owner: §9).
+- **Filters reorganized into a clean compact layout** (§10), not one long line.
+- **Viewer must be in control of available screen space** (§11) and the piece must fit correctly (§15).
+- **Holes & Slots** must respect space, be resizable/minimizable/restorable, letting the viewer take full space when minimized (§12).
+
+---
+
+## 5. Current UX Problems
+
+Documented from the technical audit + source verification. These are **observations**, not a redesign mandate.
+
+1. **Main workspace splitter** is not satisfactory — resizing the file table vs viewer is awkward (owner: §7).
+2. **Project selector** shows a project name such as `STE ACTION IMMC`, with a pencil; the rename pencil currently acts on the **order**; requirement to write the rename through to the project storage folder name has to be reconciled with existing logic (owner: §8).
+3. **Search** placement not aligned with the requirement (must sit directly below the project selector) (owner: §9).
+4. **Filters** (category / family) are placed in one long horizontal line; must be a clean compact layout (owner: §10).
+5. **Viewer controls** — text like "2D Viewer" / "Faces" create visual clutter; controls must be cleaner/compact yet consistent (owner: §11).
+6. **View information clutter** — Name / Profile / Folder / Qty / Status / O / SI-EN currently shown inside the viewer; owner wants this textual clutter removed from the viewer (presentation only — underlying data stays) (owner: §12).
+7. **Holes & Slots table** too large; needs resizable/minimizable/restorable behavior + preference/default state (owner: §13).
+8. **X/Y display** elements appear fixed, not dynamic/useful — a UX problem to analyze (owner: §15).
+9. **Cursor snapping** must feel precise/smooth/predictable/professional — the engine is **not** to change in this phase; document the UX requirement only (owner: §16).
+10. **Fit / foreshorten** — piece/view must fit inside the available viewer area; current behavior makes the piece too small / cuts geometry wrongly. **Fit ≠ clipping/cutting** (owner: §17).
+11. **Themes** — only dark themes exist; need a real White/Light theme **with full consistency** across all surfaces (owner: §18).
+12. **Window design** — inconsistent title/header/body styling across windows; must unify (owner: §19).
+13. **Nesting Workspace** — currently a mockup and **must be removed** from NC MANAGER (nesting belongs to another program) (owner: §20, §24).
+14. **Measurement / cotation** — needs to become professional (multi-dimension, keep several, delete one, snapping, placement) (owner: §21).
+15. **Printing** — professional printing/PDF/direct printer/cartouche layout desired; define workflow only, do not implement (owner: §22).
+16. **Home / navigation** — no Home button; owner wants one in the upper-left giving organized access to Settings/Themes/global options (owner: §23).
+17. **Save / Open Project** placement — must be upper-left, compact (file icon + arrow/action indicator) (owner: §24).
+18. **Steel Engine** — needs a clear UI entry point to open and inspect (owner: §26) (entry exists; see §26).
