+## 6. Desired UX Principles
+
+Global, product-wide principles:
+
+- **Clear** — one obvious action per region; no ambiguity.
+- **Predictable** — same control behaves the same everywhere; state is visible.
+- **Coherent** — one visual language across every window; no unrelated-looking windows.
+- **Efficient** — common industrial actions (import, organize, inspect, edit hole, rename) reachable in minimal steps.
+- **Precise** — dimensions, snapping, and geometry display are exact and trustworthy.
+- **Industrial** — serious tone; no decorative animation that adds noise.
+- **Trustworthy** — read-only inspection is read-only; edits are explicit, undoable, dirty-flagged.
+- **Consistent** — a single theme applies to main window, dialogs, settings, import, message dialogs, controls, tables, viewer, menus, secondary windows (owner: §18).
+- **Easy to understand** — terminology matches the workshop; no invented jargon.
+- **Safe for real work** — never corrupt originals; never break storage or data contracts (§26).
+
+**Design-system principle (owner):**
+New UI elements must follow the existing application's visual language **unless there is a documented reason to improve that language**. Do not introduce random colors or random styles.
+
