+## 16. Fit / Foreshorten UX
+
+**Requirement (owner) — important existing problem:**
+- The piece/view must **fit correctly inside the available viewer area**.
+- Current behavior effectively makes the piece **too small** or **cuts the displayed geometry the wrong way**.
+- **Do NOT assume "fit to view" means clipping / cutting the actual geometry.**
+
+**Must separate these concepts (owner):**
+- **geometry**
+- **viewport**
+- **zoom**
+- **fit**
+- **clipping**
+- **foreshortening**
+
+**Definition:**
+- **Geometry** = the real, unmodified shape of the part.
+- **Viewport** = the available area in the viewer, which may be resized (§7, §13).
+- **Zoom** = magnification applied to the geometry.
+- **Fit** = choose zoom so the whole part is comfortably visible in the viewport — **without altering or clipping the geometry**.
+- **Clipping** = cutting the drawn geometry at the viewport boundary; must **not** be conflated with fit. The part belongs inside the viewport, not be clipped to fit.
+- **Foreshortening** = the visual shortening of a projected view (e.g. an angled beam appears shorter in the 2D plane); distinct from fit/zoom/clipping.
+
+**UX rule:** When the user asks the view to fit (or the viewport is resized), the entire part must be framed appropriately inside the available area — not auto-clipped in the "wrong way", and not left tiny.
+
+**`OPEN DECISION`:** Exact framing margin and whether resize auto-reframes. Not specified.
+
