+## 2. Tekla Dimension Architecture — Deep Pattern Analysis
+
+### 2.1 The "Dimension Set" Concept
+
+Tekla does NOT create individual dimension objects. It creates **Dimension Sets**:
+
+```
+DimensionSet {
+    direction_line: (p1, p2)     ← the line along which dimensions are measured
+    points: [(x1,y1), (x2,y2), ...]  ← measurement points
+    offset: float                 ← distance of dimension line from the part
+    style: DimensionStyle         ← text height, arrow type, precision
+}
+```
+
+A Dimension Set = a chain of collinear measurements along a reference direction.
+
+**Key insight:** The reference line defines the measurement axis. All points are projected onto this axis.
+
+### 2.2 The 3-Click Workflow
+
+Every Tekla dimension follows the same pattern:
+
+```
+Click 1: Origin/first measurement point
+Click 2..N: Additional measurement points (chain continues)
+Middle-click (or Esc): Place the dimension line at current offset
+```
+
+**The offset** = perpendicular distance from the part to the dimension line. The middle-click position determines this offset.
+
+### 2.3 Projection Model
+
+For **Perpendicular** and **Parallel** dimensions:
+
+```
+Given: reference line L defined by (A, B)
+Given: measurement point P
+
+Perpendicular: project P onto line perpendicular to L through A
+               → dimension measures distance along L's normal
+
+Parallel: project P onto line L itself
+          → dimension measures distance along L's direction
+```
+
+### 2.4 Orthogonal Auto-Detection
+
+```
+Given: set of points to dimension
+Compute: dx = max(x) - min(x), dy = max(y) - min(y)
+If dx > dy: use Horizontal
+Else: use Vertical
+```
+
+### 2.5 Arc Dimension
+
+```
+Given: 3 points P1, P2, P3 on arc
+Compute: center C, radius R, start/end angles
+Display: arc length = R × θ (in radians)
+         OR angle in degrees
+Radial lines extend from center to arc endpoints
+```
+
