+# NC1 / DSTV — The CNC Part Description Format
+
+> **What this file is.** The project's working reference for the DSTV NC1 file
+> format (as used by Tekla Structures, Advance Steel, SDS/2 and the CNC beam
+> drill lines at the other end). This is the "brain" the feature work should
+> consult whenever hole / face / outline / marking behaviour is touched.
+>
+> Sources: the public DSTV / NC1 documentation collected from the NC1 world
+> (dstv.deutscherstahlbau.de standard, nc1-viewer.com guides, Tekla NC notes).
+
+---
+
+## 1. The short answer
+
+A DSTV **NC1 file** is a plain-text description of **one structural steel
+part**, in a machine-neutral standard format that CNC machines understand. It
+describes the profile + dimensions, steel grade, length, every hole with its
+exact position and diameter, end cuts / copes, internal openings and layout
+markings.
+
+- **DSTV** = *Deutscher Stahlbau-Verband* (German Steel Construction
+  Association) — defined the *Standard Description for Steel Structure Pieces
+  for Numerical Controls*.
+- One part per file, ASCII text, coordinates in **millimetres**, angles in
+  **degrees**.
+
+### Who produces / who consumes
+| Side | Tools |
+|---|---|
+| Produce (exporters) | Tekla Structures, Autodesk Advance Steel, SDS/2, add-ins for Inventor / SolidWorks / AutoCAD |
+| Consume (machines) | Ficep / Peddinghaus / Kaltenbach / Voortman beam drill + saw-drill lines, plasma / oxy / laser plate cutters, MRP nesting software |
+
+---
+
+## 2. Anatomy — blocks
+
+Every file is a sequence of **blocks**, each introduced by a two-letter code
+alone on a line; data lines follow indented, until the next block code.
+
+| Code | German | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `ST` | Start | Part header: identifiers, grade, profile, dimensions |
+| `EN` | Ende | End of the part description |
+| `BO` | Bohrung | Holes (incl. slots, counterbores, stepped/blind/threaded) |
+| `AK` | Außenkontur | Outer contour: end cuts, copes, miters, bevels |
+| `IK` | Innenkontur | Inner contour: openings and cut-outs |
+| `SI` | Signierung | Numbering: piece-mark text on the part (hard stamping) |
+| `PU` | Pulver | Powder / scribe marking: layout lines, polylines |
+| `KO` | Körner | Punch (center-punch) marks, points / point sequences |
+| `KA` | Kanten | Bend lines (mainly plates / sheet) |
+| `SC` | Schnitt | Saw cut information |
+| `TO` | Toleranz | Tolerances |
+| `UE` | Überhöhung | Camber |
+| `PR` / `IN` | Profil / Information | Profile data / free-form info (rarely used) |
+
+### Face codes — v, o, u, h
+Data lines in `BO`, `SI`, `PU`, `KO`, `AK`, `IK` begin with a lowercase face
+letter:
+
+| Letter | Face | `I`-beam meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `v` | front / web (Vorderseite) | web, drilled from +y |
+| `o` | top (Oben) | top flange, from +z |
+| `u` | bottom (Unten) | bottom flange, from -z |
+| `h` | rear (Hinten) | back of web / back side of a channel-angle |
+
+**Coordinates are per-face**: `X` runs along the member from the start end;
+`Y` is measured within the described face (web height on `v`/`h`, flange width
+on `o`/`u`). The same physical hole shows different Y on different faces —
+this is the single biggest source of confusion when reading raw files, and the
+reason a viewer should always show faces separately.
+
+> The classic 4-face wording is authoritative in this project
+> (`core/face_planes.py`): v=web front, h=rear web, o=top flange, u=bottom
+> flange. **Real L-programs in this repo only ever carry v and u features**
+> (never h/o) — the face list exposed in the hole editor is profile-aware.
+
+---
+
+## 3. ST — the header
+
+One per file, everything depends on it. One value per line, in order:
+order identification, drawing number, **phase**, **piece/position mark**,
+**steel grade** (e.g. S235), **quantity**, **profile type code** (see §5),
+**profile name** (e.g. W14X90, HEA200), then the numeric dimensions: length,
+profile height, flange width, flange thickness, web thickness, radius,
+weight/metre, painting surface, web/flange start angles for pre-cut ends.
+
+The **profile type code** decides how every coordinate that follows is
+interpreted — get it wrong and a web hole lands on a flange.
+
+---
+
+## 4. BO — holes, slots, counterbores
+
+Each line: `face X Y diameter [extra fields]`.
+
+```text
+BO
+  v  200.00  95.00  18.00
+  o  100.00u 50.00  14.00  10.00  40.00  0.00
+```
+
+- **Plain hole** — `face X Y d` (3 numbers).
+- **Slotted hole** — a slot length follows (or engine marker tokens). In this
+  project the real files use a 7-token form
+  `face X Y d <marker> L angle1 angle2`, e.g.
+  `u 36.00o 40.25 22.00 0.00l 18.00 0.00 0.00` → `d=22`, slot `L=18`,
+  `angle=0`. The `l` suffix on the 4th token marks that a slot length follows.
+  The `o`/`s`/`u` suffix letters on coordinates are **reference-edge
+  markers** (exporter-dependent) — parse by tokens, ignore as numbers.
+- **Counterbore / stepped hole** — a larger shallow bore + through hole at the
+  same position expressed as a paired definition; a robust parser recombines
+  the pair into one physical hole.
+- **Suffix letters on coordinates** (`u`, `o`, `s`…) indicate the reference
+  edge used for measuring — another exporter quirk.
+
+> **Project rule (fixed, verified):** the NC slot `length` is the
+> **centre-to-centre** distance of the two end radii (real files: `d=22`,
+> `length=18`, i.e. `length < diameter`). Overall slot extent = `length + d`
+> (here `40 x 22` mm). When a renderer takes an "overall length", pass
+> `length + d` (see the B-Rep note in §12).
+
+---
+
+## 5. Profile types
+
+Line 8 of the ST header. Decides which faces exist and how coordinates map.
+
+| Code | Shape | Typical sections |
+|---|---|---|
+| `I` | I/H sections (beams, columns) | IPE, HEA/HEB/HEM, W-shapes, UB/UC |
+| `U` | Channels | UPN/UPE, C and MC, PFC |
+| `L` | Angles | equal + unequal leg angles |
+| `B` | Plate / flat (Blech) | base plates, gussets, stiffeners, flats |
+| `RO` | Round tube (Rohr) | CHS, pipe |
+| `RU` | Round bar (Rundstahl) | solid rounds, anchor rods |
+| `M` | Rectangular / square hollow | RHS, SHS, HSS tube |
+| `C` | Cold-formed C profiles | purlins |
+| `T` | T-sections | rolled tees, split beams |
+| `SO` | Special / other | anything not covered (Z-profiles, etc.) |
+
+### How faces map onto each shape
+- **I**: v=web, o/u=top/bottom flanges. Flange holes measure Y across flange
+  width; web holes Y up profile height.
+- **U / C**: like I but the web sits at one side; **h addresses the back of
+  the web** (a face an I-beam does not drill).
+- **L (angles)**: two legs — typically v for one leg and o *or* u for the
+  other, with the heel as reference. **The real files in this repo use v+u.**
+- **B (plates)**: one working face (v); AK carries the outer contour; KA bend
+  lines may appear for folded plates.
+- **M / RO**: four faces for rectangular tube; round tube effectively unrolled
+  along the circumference; through-drilling both walls = holes on opposite
+  faces.
+- **T**: a web + a single flange → v and one of o/u.
+
+### Header dimensions that go with the code
+After profile code + name: length, height, width, flange thickness, web
+thickness, radius, weight per metre. Machines trust these numbers over any
+catalog lookup — the file must stand alone. A good viewer cross-checks them
+against a section database and flags inconsistencies.
+
+### Practical gotchas
+- **Plates exported as I**: some add-ins export everything as I-profiles with
+  zero flange thickness.
+- **RO vs RU**: tube vs solid bar — one wall-thickness field separates them.
+- **Imperial names, metric numbers**: `W14X90` still carries mm dimensions;
+  the name is only a label.
+- **Unequal angles**: which leg the exporter treats as `v` differs per
+  package.
+
+---
+
+## 6. AK and IK — outer / inner contours
+
+- **AK** traces the outer boundary of a face as a polyline of X/Y vertices —
+  end cuts, copes, miters, bevels. A third value on a vertex line is a
+  **bulge/radius** indicating an arc to the next vertex (curved copes,
+  radiused corners).
+- **IK** uses the same vertex syntax for non-round openings: rectangular
+  openings, plasma-cut slots, irregular cut-outs. A face can have multiple IK
+  contours.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Marking blocks — SI, PU, KO
+
+| Block | What it is | Typical use |
+|---|---|---|
+| `SI` | Numbering / hard stamping — a **text string** on the part | piece marks, shop numbers, heat numbers |
+| `PU` | Powder / scribe marking — **polylines** on the surface | layout for stiffeners, gussets, connection outlines |
+| `KO` | Center-punch marks — **points** or point sequences | hole centres before drilling, discrete layout points |
+
+The shop-floor distinction that matters: **SI produces characters; PU and KO
+produce geometry.** A machine that can scribe a line may not write text and
+vice versa.
+
+### SI — piece marks
+Per line: `face X Y angle text-height text`.
+Two optional extras in real files: a single reference letter between face and
+X (dimension reference, non-numeric), and a short flag token of letters
+(`x`/`z`) before the text (rotation reference / parameter handling). Both are
+easy to mistake for part of the text. Text height is not decorative — it
+selects the stamping die / depth.
+
+### PU — powder / scribe layout
+Same line format as AK/IK: `face X Y radius` per vertex (open polyline).
+Two rules:
+1. **Marking polylines need not be closed** — closing them invents a segment.
+2. A **new block header ends the current marking run**; two consecutive PU
+   blocks are two separate polylines.
+
+### KO — punch marks
+Same format as PU; intent is center-punching. Single-point = one spot;
+multi-point = a series.
+
+---
+
+## 8. Blocks with numbered plane variants
+
+Some exporters emit numbered plane variants instead of (or as well as) the
+classic codes — e.g. `E1/E2` for contours, `B1` for holes on a numbered plane.
+A robust parser maps plane-suffixed blocks (`E`, `B`, `S`, `A`, `I`, `P`, `K`
++ digit) back onto the standard block semantics so the file renders regardless
+of dialect.
+
+---
+
+## 9. Exporter quirks to expect from real files
+
+1. Different token spacing / padding — **parse by tokens, not columns**.
+2. Reference-edge suffix letters on coordinates (`u`, `o`, `s`…).
+3. Counterbores split across lines that must be recombined.
+4. Numbered plane blocks (`E1`, `B1`) instead of classic codes.
+5. Missing optional fields that some machines require.
+
+---
+
+## 10. Quality checklist (what the viewer/editor should verify)
+
+- Header sanity: piece mark vs drawing, grade, quantity, profile name ↔
+  dimensions consistency.
+- Length vs cut list (millimetres; watch for imperial-name/metric-number).
+- Hole count / pattern per face; gauge (cross-member spacing) and pitch
+  (along-member spacing) on bolt groups.
+- Hole sizes: bolt holes are usually 2–3 mm over bolt diameter (metric
+  practice) or 1/16″ over (imperial) — so `Ø18` for an M16 bolt is expected,
+  `Ø16` is a problem. Slots must show their full length AND width.
+- Copes and end cuts on the correct flange for the connection orientation.
+- Openings match the service-opening schedule.
+- Markings: SI present + on a visible face; PU/KO where fit-up needs them.
+- **Mirrored parts**: handed pairs must really be mirrored, not copied.
+
+> **Duplicates:** Tekla ignores duplicate bolts (same location as another) by
+> default; tolerance adjustable with XS_BOLT_DUPLICATE_TOLERANCE.
+> **Unfolded plates:** check zero-weight parts — they cannot be unfolded; and
+> neutral-axis parameters are needed to unfold profiles correctly.
+
+---
+
+## 11. Related formats
+
+- **NC1 vs DXF**: DXF is generic 2D/3D drawing geometry; NC1 is one-part
+  manufacturing data with fabrication semantics machines act on directly.
+- **NC1 vs IFC**: IFC models a whole building; NC1 describes a single piece.
+- **NC1 vs proprietary CNC**: vendor controller formats exist, but nearly all
+  machines import DSTV — it is the common denominator.
+
+---
+
+## 12. Project mapping (how this repo implements the format)
+
+| Format concept | Location |
+|---|---|
+| Parser producing the `holes`/`contours`/`markings` list (canonical convention) | `src/nccorrection/core/viewer.py` (`parse_geometry`, slot branch) |
+| Header-compatible engine parser (BO slot fix now matches canonical) | `src/nccorrection/engine/parser/dstv_parser.py` (`_parse_bo_line`) |
+| Face frames → real 3D surfaces; per-profile face sets | `src/nccorrection/core/face_planes.py` (`real_faces`, `frame`, `convert`) |
+| Write hole edits back to NC1 text | `src/nccorrection/core/nc1_writer.py` (`apply_hole_edits`, `_format_feature`) |
+| 3D hole + slot placements on the solid | `src/nccorrection/core/part3d.py` (`_hole_placements`, `_slot_placements`, `slot_rims`) |
+| 2D beam view (slots, rims, centre crosses) | `src/nccorrection/ui/qt_viewer.py` |
+| 3D view (hole rims, slot outlines, crosses) | `src/nccorrection/ui/qt_viewer_3d.py` |
+| Face / blueprint 2D engine renderer | `src/nccorrection/engine/render_2d/renderer_2d.py` + `blueprint_palette` |
+| Plane switching / copying feature to another face | `src/nccorrection/core/plane_switcher.py` |
+| Theme-merged face palette (developed faces + 2D dark look) | `src/nccorrection/design/theme.py` (`blueprint_palette`, theme-aware) |
+| 2D/3D engines + face renderer share the measure palette | `src/nccorrection/design/theme.py` (`measure_palette`) |
+
+**Working conventions to keep in mind when editing:**
+- Slots are explicit **B-Rep**: `_build_slot_path` / `_slot_loop` use tangent
+  lines + explicit arcs (never a rounded-rect shortcut) so future Nesting
+  (Clipper/GEOS) can parse the exact boundary. These functions take the
+  **overall** length (`NC length + d`); the straight part between the two
+  drill centres is `overall - d`. Renderers pass `length + d` at the call
+  site (e.g. `d=22, length=18` → overall `40` → stadium `40 x 22` mm).
+- The 3D `_slot_loop` left cap must bulge **outward** (`-half_len - r*cos`),
+  mirroring the right cap, or the slot renders 29 wide instead of 40.
+- The profile type must drive both the real faces and the coordinate frames —
+  never hard-code the full v/h/o/u set for L-plates.
+- Always add a crosshair at slot/hole centres that scales with zoom (2D, 3D,
+  face views) — "a single line is not enough".
+- Verify with the real files under `projects/01-CN/input/*.nc1`, and keep the
+  full test suite green.
+
+**UI / theme merge conventions (current phase):**
+- The Blueprint toggle shows the *developed faces* through the exact same
+  face renderer, but its palette now derives from the active app theme:
+  `blueprint_palette(theme)` returns a light "paper" sheet when called with no
+  (dark) theme and a dark navy/cyan/amber/rose sheet when given a dark theme
+  dict. So the blueprint reads like the 2D beam view — one look everywhere.
+- `blueprint_palette()` **with no argument must keep the light industrial
+  values** (`#f0f3f4`, `#2980b9`, `#e67e22`) — several tests pin them.
+- Dimension styling is AutoCAD-like: `_dim_arrows` draws a solid filled
+  arrowhead (direction via the `along` unit vector), and auto-dim labels are
+  drawn in transparent-normal mode with an opaque `paper` backing so dim
+  lines never cut through the text.
+- All three viewers get the **same** `measure_palette` from the app, so the
+  measure ghost/readout matches across 2D / 3D / faces.
+
+**Snap-engine conventions (current phase):**
+- Snap math lives in the pure leaf engine `src/nccorrection/engine/math/osnap.py`
+  (no Qt); `design/snaps.py` re-exports it under the names the viewers use.
+- `OsnapTargets.arcs` carries directed arc rims `(cx, cy, r, a0_deg, a1_deg)`
+  (CCW). Slots feed their **true B-Rep stadium** via `stadium_rim(cx, cy, width,
+  overall, angle)` (two tangent walls + two semicircular caps) so `nearest` /
+  `midpoint` / `tangent` / `intersection` snap exactly onto the drawn 40 x 22
+  slot rims — line-arc intersections are filtered to the arc's angular span so
+  buried half-arcs never report a crossing.
+- `resolve_osnap(..., exact_band=...)` is two-band: within `exact_band` the
+  highest-priority kind wins (sticky endpoints near corners); outside it the
+  closest point wins. Default `exact_band=0.0` preserves the historical
+  closest-point rule; the design layer defaults the band to `4.0`.
+- **Dead-on rule** (`_DEAD_BAND = 0.5` in the same units as the targets): a
+  candidate within `_DEAD_BAND` of the cursor is a dead-on hit and always wins,
+  regardless of band or priority. So clicking the middle of a slot returns
+  `centre` (the slot-centre circle) instead of a wall/cap `intersection` that
+  happens to sit in the band a few px away; clicking on a rim/vertex returns
+  `nearest`/`endpoint` on that exact point.
+- `polar_snap(p1, cursor, increment, tol)` implements AutoCAD-style polar
+  tracking: the cursor is orthogonally projected onto the nearest multiple of
+  the increment (default 15 deg, tol 2 deg) when close enough. `qt_viewer`
+  `_snap_ortho` now returns `(point, angle, is_ortho, polar_deg)` and the live
+  measure preview snap-locks to the ray and tags the readout `[polar]`; the
+  face renderer applies the same lock to its preview when no OSNAP is active.
+- New `DesignSettings` keys: `polar_enabled` (default True), `polar_angle`
+  (15), `polar_tol` (2), `snap_exact_band` (4). When OSNAP master is off the
+  engine still receives an empty kind set (no object snap at all).
+- All three viewers pass `exact_band` through `resolve_snap`; targets are
+  built in viewer-local space (canvas px for the face renderer, scene mm for
+  the 2D/3D viewers) — the same convention as `snap_tol`.
